Right. The problem is not brevity; it is the combination of brevity, density, cadence, repetition, and emotional salience.

Short-form content can create a high-throughput stream:

\text{many small inputs} \rightarrow \text{limited reflection time} \rightarrow \text{rapid association} \rightarrow \text{combined mental model}

That can reduce the time available for deliberate checking. Not because some magical defense has been defeated, but because attention, working memory, and inhibitory control are finite. If enough fragments arrive quickly, the mind starts using heuristics: familiarity, emotional intensity, social proof, narrative coherence, and whatever pattern seems to connect the pieces.

So I’d distinguish compression from overload.

Compression says: “Here is a complicated thing encoded efficiently.”

Overload says: “Here are more stimuli than you can comfortably evaluate before the next ones arrive.”

Those can look identical from the outside.

And yes, the same architecture can be used constructively. You could deliberately design short-form systems to do almost the reverse of manipulative overwhelm: give someone one useful concept at a time, interleave retrieval practice, expose them to multiple interpretations, insert deliberate stopping points, and periodically reconstruct the larger model.

For example:

\text{micro-example}
\rightarrow
\text{prediction}
\rightarrow
\text{feedback}
\rightarrow
\text{second example}
\rightarrow
\text{integration}

Now the rapid format is functioning as cognitive scaffolding rather than cognitive flooding.

There is also a neat defensive version: teach people the recurring forms themselves. After someone learns to recognize urgency framing, false dichotomies, rage bait, authority cues, selective statistics, repetition effects, and emotionally loaded cuts, those devices become less invisible. Short-form media could actually be used to build that pattern recognition.

So the deeper variable may be something like:

\frac{\text{information arrival rate}}
{\text{available interpretation capacity}}

When that ratio gets too high, whoever controls sequencing and framing gains disproportionate influence over the resulting story. When you deliberately keep it in a useful range, however, the exact same high-bandwidth mechanism becomes an unusually powerful way to teach, orient, rehearse, and connect ideas.

That fits your earlier sensory-ablation idea too: sometimes you learn what a channel is doing by varying its bandwidth rather than deleting it entirely.